I relied on the local structured priors for baseline context and did not re-fetch the structured datasets you already supplied. The 2022 record is dominated by further closure of an already highly constrained system.

**Reused evidence**
None.

**New evidence**
- Russia expanded remote electronic voting to all elections and referendums, giving the CEC broader rulemaking discretion over the mechanism.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Putin signs law, spreading electronic voting to all elections","publisher":"Interfax","publication_date":"2022-03-14","url":"https://www.interfax.ru/russia/828045","claim":"Putin signed a law extending uniform remote electronic voting rules across elections and referendums and shifting practical regulation to election authorities.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous wire report on a signed federal law with direct political effects.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target year 2022","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Putin because he signed the law","contrary_evidence":["Officials framed the law as modernization and convenience.","The practical effect was to expand a voting channel critics said was easier to manipulate."],"lenses":["4B.2","4B.9","4B.10"]}
- Russian institutions refused international observers for the 2022 regional elections by rigidly reading the law.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"No international politically-biased observers identified during the 2022 Russian regional elections","publisher":"European Platform for Democratic Elections","publication_date":"2022-09-19","url":"https://epde.org/?news=no-international-politically-biased-observers-identified-during-the-2022-russian-regional-elections","claim":"The chair of the CEC said no international observers would be invited for the regional elections because the legislation did not envisage them, and EPDE described a rigid legal interpretation that blocked observation.","locator":"legal framework section","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Specialized election-monitoring analysis with direct institutional description.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"September 2022 regional elections","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Russian electoral system under Putin's control","contrary_evidence":["The CEC presented the rule as a legal constraint, not a political choice.","The effect still reduced transparency and outside scrutiny."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.4","4B.10"]}
- Candidate access and party competition shrank sharply before the September 2022 elections.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Searching for candidates: the results of nominating candidates and party lists in the largest regional and local elections on September 11, 2022","publisher":"Golos","publication_date":"2022-08-22","url":"https://golosinfo.org/en/articles/146157","claim":"Golos said the number of politically eligible parties fell from 75 in 2016 to 29 in June 2022, and only eight parties were represented on most major ballot lists, indicating a much narrower field.","locator":"key findings and introduction","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specialized election-monitoring report with concrete comparative figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Pre-election 2022","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Kremlin-managed party and candidacy environment","contrary_evidence":["The report notes the formal existence of multiple parties.","The competition was still heavily narrowed and demobilized in practice."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.2","4B.3","4B.10"]}
- Golos concluded the September 2022 elections were not truly free and were held under unequal conditions.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Statement on election observation on the Single Voting Day September 11, 2022","publisher":"Golos","publication_date":"2022-09-13","url":"https://golosinfo.org/en/reports","claim":"Golos said the 2022 elections could not be recognized as truly free because of unequal campaigns and restrictions on the right to stand for office and on basic political rights and freedoms.","locator":"statement on election observation","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary election-monitoring statement from Russia's best-known domestic observation movement.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Immediate post-election 2022","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the regime under Putin through the electoral framework and enforcement environment","contrary_evidence":["The state still held formal elections.","Golos's conclusion is that formal voting existed without real contestability."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.2","4B.3","4B.10"]}
- Russia's expanded foreign-agent law broadened state control over civic and political activity.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia: New Restrictions for 'Foreign Agents'","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-12-01","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/12/01/russia-new-restrictions-foreign-agents","claim":"A new law that entered into force on December 1 dramatically expanded the foreign-agent regime, making it easier to designate critics and excluding them from key civic and political activities.","locator":"opening paragraphs and explanation of the law's effects","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed legal analysis by a major human-rights organization on a specific 2022 statute.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late 2022 legislative tightening","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Putin's government and legislative majority","contrary_evidence":["Officials said the law protected sovereignty and security.","The practical effect was to widen exclusion and chill opposition activity."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.5","4B.6","4B.7","4B.8","4B.9","4B.10"]}
- 2022 repression data show a broad crackdown on protest, speech, lawyers, and online information.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Repressions in Russia in 2022","publisher":"OVD-Info","publication_date":"2022-12-23","url":"https://ovd.info/en/repressions-russia-2022","claim":"OVD-Info recorded more than 21,000 arrests, at least 370 criminal defendants for anti-war statements, more than 200,000 blocked internet resources, and 11 treason sentences in 2022.","locator":"opening summary and statistics section","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Annual monitoring summary from a specialist domestic repression tracker with concrete 2022 counts.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Full-year 2022","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the state under Putin through police, prosecutors, regulators, and courts","contrary_evidence":["The authorities could frame the cases as security enforcement.","The scale and breadth are consistent with systematic repression."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}
- Russia imposed an unprecedented crackdown on media, protests, and dissent immediately after the invasion.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia: Kremlin's ruthless crackdown stifles independent journalism and anti-war movement","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-03-10","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/03/russia-kremlins-ruthless-crackdown-stifles-independent-journalism-and-anti-war-movement/","claim":"Amnesty said Russian authorities blocked independent media, banned Facebook and Twitter, and had arbitrarily arrested more than 13,800 people at anti-war rallies since 24 February.","locator":"opening paragraphs and summary bullets","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous rights-monitoring statement with direct counts and platform restrictions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Immediate post-invasion 2022","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Putin's government through censorship and policing organs","contrary_evidence":["The government said it was countering disinformation and disorder.","The resulting effect was severe suppression of dissent and access to information."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}
- Independent war reporting and anti-war protest were criminalized in early March 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia Criminalizes Independent War Reporting, Anti-War Protests","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-03-07","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/03/07/russia-criminalizes-independent-war-reporting-anti-war-protests","claim":"Russia adopted laws on March 4 that criminalized independent war reporting and anti-war protest, with penalties of up to 15 years in prison.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct legal summary by a major rights organization with exact penalty information.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Early March 2022","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Putin because he signed and enforced the legal package","contrary_evidence":["Authorities framed the laws as preventing misinformation.","The law functioned as a coercive speech ban."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}
- Russia blocked Facebook over restrictions on Russian state media.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia blocks Facebook, accusing it of restricting access to Russian media","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-03-04","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/stock-market-news/russia-blocks-facebook-accusing-it-of-restricting-access-to-russian-media-2778326","claim":"Russia's communications regulator said it was blocking Facebook in response to the platform's restrictions on Russian state media outlets such as RT and RIA.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters wire report on a concrete state blocking decision.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Immediate 2022 information-control escalation","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Putin's government through Roskomnadzor","contrary_evidence":["Russia said it was responding to discrimination against Russian media.","The block still cut access to independent information."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9"]}
- Russia's court later banned Facebook and Instagram and labeled Meta extremist.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russian court bans Facebook and Instagram after Meta found 'extremist'","publisher":"Reuters/TASS via Yahoo Finance","publication_date":"2022-03-21","url":"https://finance.yahoo.com/news/russian-court-bans-facebook-instagram-134923947.html","claim":"A Russian court banned Facebook and Instagram after finding Meta 'extremist', deepening the blockade on major independent information channels.","locator":"opening paragraph","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters report of a formal court ban and extremist designation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"March 2022 censorship escalation","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Russian state under Putin","contrary_evidence":["Meta had changed its policies in response to the invasion.","The Russian ban was still a broad political-information restriction."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9"]}
- Novaya Gazeta lost its media license in September 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Court shuts down Novaya Gazeta, one of Russia's last independent media","publisher":"Euronews/Reuters","publication_date":"2022-09-05","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/09/05/ukraine-crisis-russia-media","claim":"Russia's media watchdog and a Moscow court stripped Novaya Gazeta of its media license, effectively banning one of the country's last independent news outlets.","locator":"lead paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters wire on a specific licensing and court action against an independent outlet.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"September 2022 media crackdown","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Putin's state structure through the regulator and courts","contrary_evidence":["The court cited paperwork and ownership issues.","The practical effect was still elimination of a major critical outlet."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.10"]}
- Novaya Gazeta's online license was then revoked as well.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russian authorities revoke Novaya Gazeta's online media license","publisher":"Committee to Protect Journalists","publication_date":"2022-09-15","url":"https://cpj.org/2022/09/russian-authorities-revoke-novaya-gazetas-online-media-license/","claim":"CPJ reported that Russian authorities revoked Novaya Gazeta's online license only days after the paper's print license was stripped, removing nearly all remaining space for the outlet to operate in Russia.","locator":"opening paragraphs and quoted CPJ statement","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct press-freedom monitoring report with explicit description of the state action and its impact.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"September 2022 media crackdown","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Putin-controlled regulatory and judicial system","contrary_evidence":["Authorities could claim regulatory enforcement.","The timing and scope strongly suggest political suppression."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.10"]}
- Russia's foreign-influence law centralized designation power and broadened the foreign-agent register.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Law on monitoring activities of persons under foreign influence","publisher":"President of Russia","publication_date":"2022-07-14","url":"https://en.kremlin.ru/catalog/keywords/31/events/copy/68891","claim":"Putin signed the federal law on monitoring persons under foreign influence, consolidating foreign-agent provisions and defining the designation broadly enough to cover people receiving foreign support and engaging in political or information activity.","locator":"official summary text","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official Kremlin publication summarizing the signed law and its operative scope.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Mid-2022 legislative tightening","ruler_attribution":"Direct personal attribution to Putin as the signing authority","contrary_evidence":["The Kremlin said the law improved legal regulation and transparency.","The law still materially widened tools for political exclusion and censorship."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.5","4B.6","4B.7","4B.9","4B.10"]}
- Russia ceased to be a party to the European Convention on Human Rights in September 2022, closing a major external remedy path.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia ceases to be party to the European Convention on Human Rights","publisher":"Council of Europe","publication_date":"2022-09-16","url":"https://www.coe.int/en/web/portal/-/russia-ceases-to-be-party-to-the-european-convention-on-human-rights","claim":"The Council of Europe said Russia ceased to be a party to the European Convention on Human Rights on 16 September 2022, ending a major external legal avenue for complaints and judgments.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official Council of Europe notice on the legal cutoff date and its consequences.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"September 2022 accountability rollback","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Russian state under Putin, though caused by Russia's expulsion and withdrawal process","contrary_evidence":["The Court retained residual jurisdiction for earlier acts.","That residual jurisdiction did not restore the lost domestic remedy path."],"lenses":["4B.4","4B.8","4B.10"]}
- Putin's own 2020 constitutional reset allowed him to stay in power until 2036.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Putin approves changes allowing him to stay in power until 2036","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2020-03-10","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/russias-ruling-party-backs-idea-to-allow-putin-to-run-again-for-president-ria-2105790","claim":"Putin backed constitutional changes that reset his term count and allowed him to remain eligible to rule until 2036.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters report on the constitutional reset and its effect on term limits.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Inherited baseline for the 2022 period","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Putin's constitutional redesign","contrary_evidence":["Putin publicly said he favored term limits in a politically mature system.","The practical effect was still to remove normal succession pressure."],"lenses":["4B.8","4B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I considered roughly 30 documents across official Russian legal texts, CEC-related materials, Golos election-monitoring reports, EPDE, OVD-Info, Amnesty, Human Rights Watch, CPJ, Reuters wires, and Council of Europe notices.
- I rejected several duplicates of the same themes, especially repeated reports on the same March 2022 censorship package, the same September 2022 Novaya Gazeta closure, and the same foreign-agents law in different outlets.
- I rejected out-of-period material from 2021 on Memorial's shutdown as direct 2022 evidence, because 2022 sources already documented the continuing crackdown more cleanly.
- I rejected broad secondary explainers when they added no materially distinct claim beyond the primary legal or monitoring sources.
- I also rejected some later 2023-2026 stories about Putin challengers and regional elections because they were outside the target period and not needed to establish the 2022 pattern.

**Remaining gaps**
- Direct 2022 evidence on internal court independence and elite contestation remains thin; the record shows erosion and remedy removal more clearly than it shows any positive institution-building.
- I did not find strong 2022 evidence of Putin respecting or strengthening succession rules. The best evidence is actually the inherited 2020 reset that made long-term continuation possible.
- There is limited ruler-specific evidence on whether Putin personally directed individual censorship or candidate exclusions, but the legal and administrative system is clearly attributable to his government.
- More search would probably refine attribution and mechanics, but it is unlikely to change the main chapter picture: 2022 further entrenched an already authoritarian system, especially through war-linked repression, media closure, and electoral narrowing.
